Topographical map performance is fixed. Here’s what changed and why it matters.

## Root cause
The old builder sampled **every display pixel** with multiple world queries per cell (`get_tile_type`, `get_surface_height`, `get_biome`, feature/spawn/town/crystal lookups). A fullscreen 512×512 map could process **8k+ expensive samples per frame**, producing 1s+ spikes.

## Optimizations

### `systems/topographical_map_builder.gd`
- **Lower internal resolution** (`map_internal_divisor`) — sample at half (or third) resolution, upscale with nearest-neighbor on display
- **Adaptive stride** — coarser world sampling when internal size is small
- **Column cache** — terrain colors cached per world cell
- **Precomputed markers** — towns, ruins, spawns painted once (via `get_active_spawns()`)
- **Crystal bbox dict** — `get_depth_cells_in_rect()` instead of per-pixel crystal queries
- **Pixel + time budgets** — `process_job(pixel_budget, time_budget_us)` caps work per frame
- **Incremental recenter** — scroll existing image, repaint only the exposed strip
- **Crystal-only refresh** — `repaint_crystal_overlay()` updates only crystal-affected pixels when terrain is idle

### `crystal/crystal_manager.gd`
- Added `get_depth_cells_in_rect(bounds)` for fast crystal overlay lookup

### `ui/topographical_map.gd`
- Wired pixel/time budgets and internal divisor from performance config
- Tries incremental scroll before full rebuild
- Keeps idle minimap job state for scroll + crystal refresh
- Separate crystal overlay timer (terrain rebuilds less often)

### `config/performance_quality_config.gd` presets

