Crystal sim cost at ~600 cells was dominated by two things: **every cell “changing” every tick** (equilibrium jitter) and **full chunk mesh rebuilds** for all of them. Here’s what changed.

## Root cause
At ~600 cells, pressure equalization nudged depth on almost every cell (>0.02 epsilon). That drove:
- `crystal_changed_cells` ≈ 600 (misleading gauge — really “all sim writes”)
- Many chunks marked dirty per tick → expensive `crystal_mesh` + high `main_thread`

Damping also started at 600, so spread wasn’t throttled until you were already in trouble.

## Changes

### Smoother, cheaper spread (`crystal_fluid_sim.gd`)
- **Frontier-first flow** — cells with empty neighbors get priority; interior cells rotate in slowly
- **Scaled flow cap** — at 600 cells, ~220 configured cap becomes ~80 cells/tick
- **Earlier easing damping** — quadratic ramp from `spread_damping_start` (MEDIUM: 320 cells)
- **Lateral spread off** when damped (`spread_mult < 0.55`)
- **Split epsilons** — sim writes at 0.03, mesh updates at 0.20+ (`mesh_depth_epsilon`)
- **Hard new-cell cap** preserved with sorted frontier priority

### Mesh optimization (`crystal_chunk_layer.gd`, `crystal_manager.gd`)
- **`patch_cells()`** — updates only changed MultiMesh instances (no full chunk rebuild)
- **Mesh dirty tracking** — only new cells, removals, or depth Δ ≥ epsilon trigger visual updates
- **Full rebuild** only for new cells in a chunk or cleared cells
- **Profiler gauge** `crystal_changed_cells` now reports **mesh-dirty count**, not sim writes

